2 CONT ◦From the history written by Janet Kari Johnson in 1950:
2 CONT
2 CONT  0le Osland was born in 1843, died in 1920. He was the oldest son of Lars and Osland. He made the trip on the boat across the Atlantic, leaving Stavanger, Norway, May 11 1850. Besides his parents the other three children in the fa
2 CONC mily with them were Knud, Brita, and Kari Cecilie. As a young man he was below the age to serve in the Civil War, but Ole was truly a son of a pioneer. He with a group of other young men did Indian Scouting, preparatory to the att
2 CONC ack on Ft. Atkinson, while the older men, womea and children were safe in the Fort.
2 CONT  On the pioneer homestead south of Ossian he left many improvements such as fences, bridges and roads. Truly, we can say his vision was farsighted and he left a great example to his family and country. Ole Osland married Rognild G
2 CONC roth in 1872 and ltheir children had the most wonderful mother, always doing everything for the welfare of the family. Not only was the family considered, but contributions to church and community were given freety. Their home was
2 CONC  always open to give joy and comfort to all privileged to call them relatives and friends. Rognild Groth Osland was born in 1851 and died in 1938. When friends called, news of imprtance would be discussed, world events, Civil War,
2 CONC  political and social news, also the trend of fashions by the ladies. The weekly Scandinavian
2 CONT  furnished much information. Relatives of Ole Osland tell that Ole and his sister, Kari, would read of the Civil War campaigns late into the night. We could learn from these people to really read and digest our reading material. T
2 CONC he stores would also give free almanacs to the people for information. Ole Osland bought the homestead from his parents in 1874, but this place remined the home of Lars and Leva their remaining years. The farm was sold in 1909 and
2 CONC  in 1910 Ole Osland and his family moved to a farm north of Chester, Iowa. Thus ends the long trips to McGregor, Iowa and the breaking of Virgin soil by oxen and many other interesting events near Ossian, Iowa.
