2 CONT Evelyn (Jahnke) Schiff, 91, Bismarck, died Sept. 9, 2006, at Missouri Slope Lutheran Care Center, Bismarck.  Services will be held at 10:30 a.m. Saturday, Sept. 16, at New Song Church, 3200 N. 11th St., Bismarck, with the Rev. Lar
2 CONC ry Jahnke and his brother, Brian Jahnke officiating.  Burial will be in Sunset Memorial Gardens, Bismarck. Visitation will be held from 5 to 9 p.m. Thursday and from 9 a.m. to 9 p.m. Friday at Parkway Funeral Service, 2330 Tyler P
2 CONC arkway, Bismarck, where a prayer service will be held at 7 p.m. Friday.  Visitation will continue Saturday one hour prior to services at the church. Evelyn was born June 6, 1915, in Lansford, on the family farm of her parents, Joh
2 CONC n and Josie (Lekvold) Nesvig. She received her education in the Lansford area, and graduated from high school in 1933.  She continued her education at Dakota Business College in Fargo.  She met Glenmore H. Jahnke while he was work
2 CONC ing at First State Bank in Casselton.  They were married on June 20, 1939, at Durbin.  They moved to Bismarck in 1946 and began their family. Larry and Brian were fortunate children to have been born to such awesome parents.  They
2 CONC  referred to their mom as “The Greatest Mother in the Whole World,” and “Saint Evelyn,” on numerous occasions.  Glen passed away suddenly on Feb. 7, 1962m if a massive heart attack while golfing.  Evelyn was able to call 1024 N. F
2 CONC ourth St. her home for 56 years. After Glen died, Evelyn worked in the administration office for the Bismarck Public School system for 17 years.  She retired at age 65 in 1980.  That same year, she married a Fourth Street neighbor
2 CONC , whom she had known for many years.  Clem Schiff and Evelyn recently celebrated their 26th wedding anniversary on Aug. 8, 2006. Because of Evelyn’s great love for people, she had many friends everywhere she went.  She enjoyed her
2 CONC  numerous bridge groups for many years.  Her love for her God and family was shown by her actions daily.  She will be greatly missed by all. She is survived by her husband, Clem Schiff, Bismarck, her sons, Larry (Karen) Jahnke, Bi
2 CONC smarck and Brian Jahnke, Honolulu; her sister, Clara Trom, Ventura, Calif.; three grandchildren and four step-children, Marilyn Himmel, Detroit, Peggy Schiff, Denver, Robert Schiff, Dallas, and Judy (Bob) Satran, Seattle. She was 
2 CONC preceded in death by her parents, and her brothers, Leonard Nesvig, Redwood City, Calif., and Maynard Nesvig, Gaylord, Minn. Published in the Bismarck Tribune, Bismarck, North Dakota, September 13, 2006.
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/149873471 Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Aug 4 2019, 3:00:03 UTC''
2 CONT
